Installing GUI on Fedora Server
Note: Use root account or sudo for all commands

1. Available Desktop Environments
    GNOME: Default Fedora desktop - polished and user-friendly
    
    KDE Plasma: Customizable and lightweight
    
    Xfce: Fast and ideal for limited resources

2. Installation Steps
System Update

bash
sudo dnf update -y
Updates all system packages to latest versions

-y flag automatically answers "yes" to prompts

Install Desktop Environment (choose one)

    bash
    # GNOME (Full server with GUI)
    sudo dnf groupinstall "Server with GUI" -y
    
    # GNOME (Workstation version)
    sudo dnf groupinstall "Workstation" -y
    
    # KDE Plasma
    sudo dnf groupinstall "KDE Plasma Workspaces" -y
    
    # Xfce (Lightweight)
    sudo dnf groupinstall "Xfce Desktop" -y
groupinstall: Installs a complete package group, not just single packages

Each command installs desktop environment with all required components

Enable Graphical Boot

    bash
    sudo systemctl set-default graphical.target
    Sets system to boot into graphical mode by default

Changes from multi-user.target (text mode) to graphical.target

Reboot System

    bash
    sudo reboot
    Restarts the server to apply all changes

System should now boot to GUI login screen

3. Post-Installation Commands
Manual GUI Start (if needed):

    bash
    sudo systemctl start graphical.target
    Starts the GUI immediately without rebooting
    
    Use if system boots to command line instead of GUI

Install System Info Tool:

    bash
    sudo dnf install screenfetch
    screenfetch
    dnf install: Downloads and installs the screenfetch package
    
    screenfetch: Displays system information and Fedora logo in terminal

4. Package Manager Note
    DNF (used above): Modern package manager with better dependency resolution
    
    YUM: Older package manager that DNF replaced
    
    Both manage software installation, updates, and removal
